/* 運航管理の実務 Part 10 — English version */
(function(A){function set(no,en){if(A[no])A[no].en=en}
set("10-1",{"title":"The Structure and Stability of the Atmosphere: Does the Air Sink Back or Keep Rising?","hl":"atmospheric stability","subtitle":"The layers of the sky, the standard atmosphere we calculate with, why rising air cools (adiabatic change), stability and instability, and inversions: the foundation for understanding cloud, fog and turbulence","lead":["When dispatchers read a forecast, one question is always on their mind: is the air stable or unstable today? On unstable days, cumulonimbus clouds build and bring thunderstorms and severe turbulence. On stable days, fog and haze are slow to clear and airport visibility suffers.","This lesson lays the foundation for aviation weather: how the atmosphere is divided into layers, the standard atmosphere used for calculations, and what decides whether air rises or sinks back, explained with a ball and a balloon. The topic appears in almost every Korean and Japanese dispatcher exam."],"sections":[{"h":"The layers of the sky: weather happens in the lowest one","blocks":[{"t":"fig","id":"met_layers","cap":"Animated figure: the atmosphere divides into four layers by how temperature changes with height (red line). Clouds and rain form in the lowest layer, the troposphere; airliners cruise near its top and in the lower stratosphere (about 10–12 km)."},{"t":"table","cols":["Layer","Typical height","Temperature","Features and relevance to flight"],"rows":[["Troposphere","Surface to about 11 km (about 16–18 km at the equator, 8 km at the poles)","Falls with height (6.5 °C per km on average)","Almost all weather: cloud, rain, snow. The air mixes up and down"],["Stratosphere","Tropopause to about 50 km","Nearly constant low down, rising from about 20 km","The ozone layer absorbs UV and warms it. Little vertical mixing and almost no weather"],["Mesosphere","About 50–80 km","Falls with height (down to about −90 °C)","Where shooting stars glow"],["Thermosphere","Above about 80 km","Rises with height","Aurora; the International Space Station (about 400 km) orbits here"]]},{"t":"point","x":"The boundary between the troposphere and the stratosphere is the tropopause. Its height changes with latitude and season: higher at the equator than at the poles, higher in summer than in winter. Jet streams blow near the tropopause (lesson 10-3)."}]},{"h":"The International Standard Atmosphere: our measuring stick","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Real air is different every day, which makes it impossible to build altimeters or performance charts around it. So ICAO defines an average atmosphere, the International Standard Atmosphere (ISA)."},{"t":"rows","items":[{"name":"Sea-level temperature","x":"15 °C"},{"name":"Sea-level pressure","x":"1013.25 hPa (29.92 inHg)"},{"name":"Lapse rate","x":"6.5 °C per km (about 2 °C per 1,000 ft) up to 11 km (36,089 ft)"},{"name":"11–20 km","x":"Constant at −56.5 °C"}]},{"t":"point","x":"Try it: the standard temperature at 3,000 m is 15 − 6.5 × 3 = −4.5 °C. At 35,000 ft it is 15 − 2 × 35 = −55 °C, close to the constant −56.5 °C layer. How much warmer the real air is than standard (ISA+10 and so on) is used in engine-thrust and take-off performance calculations."}]},{"h":"Rising air cools: adiabatic change","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"When a parcel of air rises, the surrounding pressure is lower, so it expands. Expanding uses its own energy, so it cools without exchanging any heat. This is called adiabatic change. It is the same reason a spray can gets cold as you use it. Sinking air is squeezed and warms up."},{"t":"table","cols":["","Rate (approx.)","When"],"rows":[["Dry adiabatic lapse rate","About 10 °C per km (1 °C per 100 m, about 3 °C per 1,000 ft)","Air without cloud (unsaturated)"],["Saturated adiabatic lapse rate","About 5 °C per km (roughly 4–7 °C depending on temperature)","Air in cloud (saturated)"],["Dew point","Falls about 2 °C per km","Dew point of rising air"]]},{"t":"point","x":"Moist air cools more slowly because water vapour releases heat (latent heat) as it condenses. And since temperature falls about 10 °C per km while the dew point falls about 2 °C, the gap closes by about 8 °C per km. That gives a quick estimate of cloud base: (temperature − dew point) × about 125 m (about 400 ft). Example: 25 °C and a dew point of 17 °C give 8 × 125 = about 1,000 m."}]},{"h":"Stable and unstable: think of a ball and a balloon","blocks":[{"t":"fig","id":"met_ball","cap":"Animated figure: a ball in a valley rolls back when pushed (stable); a ball on a hilltop rolls away (unstable); a ball on flat ground stays where it stops (neutral)."},{"t":"p","x":"Atmospheric stability asks one question: if a parcel of air is lifted a little, does it return to where it started or keep going? The answer comes from comparing the parcel’s temperature with the surrounding air at the same height. Warm air is light and rises; cold air is heavy and sinks."},{"t":"fig","id":"met_parcel","cap":"Animated figure: a 20 °C balloon of air lifted from the ground cools about 10 °C per km. When the surroundings cool less (left), the balloon ends up colder and heavier and sinks back: stable. When the surroundings cool more (right), the balloon stays warmer and lighter, keeps rising and forms cloud: unstable."},{"t":"table","cols":["How fast the surroundings cool (lapse rate)","Result","Typical example"],"rows":[["Less than the saturated rate (under about 5 °C per km)","Absolutely stable","Inversions; clear winter mornings"],["Between the saturated and dry rates (about 5–10 °C)","Conditionally unstable: stable while unsaturated, unstable once saturated","The everyday atmosphere (the ISA’s 6.5 °C sits here)"],["More than the dry rate (over about 10 °C per km)","Absolutely unstable","Near the ground on a hot, sunny summer afternoon"]]},{"t":"point","x":"On a conditionally unstable day the air may be calm while it stays dry, but once mountains or a front lift it and cloud forms, cumulonimbus can build quickly. Most summer-afternoon thunderstorms follow this pattern."}]},{"h":"How stability changes the weather","blocks":[{"t":"table","cols":["","Stable air","Unstable air"],"rows":[["Cloud","Stratus, stratocumulus: spread sideways","Cumulus, cumulonimbus: build upwards"],["Precipitation","Steady (drizzle, continuous rain)","Showers, thunderstorms, hail"],["Visibility","Poor (haze, fog and smoke are trapped)","Good (except in rain)"],["Ride","Smooth (watch for shear at an inversion)","Turbulence and gusts"]]},{"t":"check","items":[{"name":"What makes air unstable","x":"Heating from below (strong sun, cold air moving over a warm sea), adding moisture low down, cold air arriving aloft"},{"name":"What makes air stable","x":"Cooling from below (night-time radiation, warm air moving over a cold sea), drying low down, warm air arriving aloft, sinking air in a high-pressure system"}]}]},{"h":"Inversions: a warm lid on the sky","blocks":[{"t":"fig","id":"met_inversion","cap":"Animated figure: normally it gets colder higher up, but in an inversion it gets warmer. The warm layer acts as a lid: smoke cannot rise and spreads sideways, and fog and haze collect beneath it. The wind often changes suddenly above and below the lid."},{"t":"table","cols":["Type","How it forms","When and where"],"rows":[["Radiation inversion","The ground loses heat at night and cools the air touching it","Clear, calm nights and mornings; the cause of radiation fog"],["Subsidence inversion","Sinking air in a high is compressed and warms","Inside highs, a few hundred to a few thousand metres up"],["Frontal inversion","Cold air below, warm air above","Near fronts"],["Turbulence inversion","A weakly mixed layer sits above a strongly mixed one","Near the ground on windy days"]]},{"t":"point","warn":true,"x":"A sudden change of wind across an inversion becomes low-level wind shear, changing the airspeed sharply during take-off or landing. And morning radiation fog will not clear until the sun warms the ground and breaks the inversion, so reading when the fog will lift feeds directly into the departure decision."}]},{"h":"How this connects to dispatch work","blocks":[{"t":"check","items":[{"name":"Morning fog","x":"A clear, calm night favours radiation fog. Check BR and FG in the TAF and when conditions improve after sunrise"},{"name":"Summer-afternoon storms","x":"Sunshine destabilises the air and cumulonimbus builds in the afternoon. Check TS and CB in the TAF, the arrival time and the alternate"},{"name":"Cold air aloft","x":"Cold air arriving aloft destabilises the atmosphere (cold lows). Watch for thunderstorms and hail in any season"},{"name":"Stability indices","x":"The Showalter Stability Index (SSI) = 500 hPa temperature − temperature of 850 hPa air lifted to 500 hPa. The more negative, the more unstable; even around +3, thunderstorms are possible if something lifts the air"}]},{"t":"link","href":"../22_学科試験過去問/学科試験_過去問.html","x":"Try the Japanese dispatcher exam past papers (aviation weather) on adiabatic change, inversions and stability"}]},{"h":"Where this appears in the exams","blocks":[{"t":"table","cols":["","Korea: flight dispatcher (aviation weather)","Japan: flight dispatcher (aviation weather)"],"rows":[["This lesson","Foundations for weather affecting flight (wind, turbulence, cloud, fog)","Structure of the atmosphere, adiabatic change, stability, inversions"],["Frequent questions","— (the questions are not published)","Types of inversion, judging conditional instability, estimating cloud base, air-mass modification (heating and cooling from below), ISA values"]]},{"t":"note","x":"* Korea’s flight dispatcher written exam (Korea Transportation Safety Authority) has five subjects: aviation law, aircraft, navigation facilities, aeronautical communications and aviation weather, each 25 questions in 30 minutes, with 70% needed per subject (checked September 2026)."}]}],"voice":"","terms":[["Troposphere","対流圏","대류권"],["Tropopause","対流圏界面","대류권계면"],["Stratosphere","成層圏","성층권"],["International Standard Atmosphere (ISA)","国際標準大気","국제표준대기"],["Lapse rate","気温減率","기온감률"],["Dry adiabatic lapse rate","乾燥断熱減率","건조단열감률"],["Saturated adiabatic lapse rate","湿潤断熱減率","습윤단열감률"],["Conditional instability","条件付き不安定","조건부 불안정"],["Inversion","逆転層","역전층"]],"quiz":[{"q":"In the International Standard Atmosphere, with 15 °C at sea level, what is the temperature at 3,000 m?","opts":["+4.5 °C","−4.5 °C","−15.0 °C","−19.5 °C"],"a":1,"exp":"The ISA lapse rate is 6.5 °C per km, so 15 − 6.5 × 3 = −4.5 °C. Using 10 °C per km gives −15 °C, but that is how fast a lifted parcel of dry air cools (the dry adiabatic lapse rate), not the standard value for the surrounding atmosphere."},{"q":"A lifted parcel of air is warmer than the surrounding air at the same height. What happens?","opts":["It returns to its starting height (stable)","It keeps rising (unstable)","It stays at that height (neutral)","It keeps sinking"],"a":1,"exp":"Warmer air is lighter than its surroundings, so buoyancy keeps it rising. That is instability, and cumulus or cumulonimbus clouds build. If it were colder it would sink back (stable); at the same temperature it would stay put (neutral)."},{"q":"With a surface temperature of 25 °C and a dew point of 17 °C, roughly how high is the base of cloud formed by rising air?","opts":["About 300 m","About 1,000 m","About 2,000 m","About 3,200 m"],"a":1,"exp":"The spread is 8 °C. Lifted air cools about 10 °C per km while its dew point falls about 2 °C, closing the gap by about 8 °C per km, so the cloud base ≈ 8 × 125 m = about 1,000 m (about 3,200 ft). 3,200 is the answer in feet, so watch the units."}],"next":"10-2 Air Masses and Fronts"});
set("10-2",{"title":"Air Masses and Fronts: Huge Bodies of Air and Where They Meet","hl":"air masses and fronts","subtitle":"The five air masses that visit Korea and Japan through the year, how air masses change on the move, the weather of cold, warm, stationary and occluded fronts, and the life of an extratropical cyclone: the core of reading a weather chart","lead":["The northwest monsoon in winter, the rainy season, the sticky heat of summer: seasonal weather is set by air masses, bodies of air thousands of kilometres across. Where air masses with different properties meet is a front, and that is where cloud, rain and turbulence gather.","Dispatchers read where fronts are and how they move on the chart, and ask when a front will pass each airport. A frontal passage swings the wind and changes the runway in use, and thunderstorms can stop departures. This lesson looks at air masses and fronts through figures and links them to the weather. It is one of the most frequently examined topics in the Japanese papers."],"sections":[{"h":"What is an air mass?","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"An air mass is a body of air thousands of kilometres across with fairly uniform temperature and humidity. It forms when air sits for a long time over a continent or ocean under light winds (a high), slowly taking on the character of the surface below. That area is its source region."},{"t":"fig","id":"met_airmass","cap":"Animated figure: five air masses visit Korea and Japan through the year. The first letter says continental (c) or maritime (m); the second polar (P), tropical (T) or equatorial (E); the third whether the air is colder (k) or warmer (w) than the surface beneath."},{"t":"table","cols":["Air mass","Code","Season","Character and weather"],"rows":[["Siberian","cPk","Winter","Cold and dry. The northwest monsoon; makes snow clouds after crossing the sea"],["Okhotsk Sea","mPk","Rainy season to early summer","Cold and moist. North of the rainy-season front; behind Korea’s Nopsae wind and Japan’s cold Yamase"],["Ogasawara (North Pacific in Korea)","mTw","Summer","Hot and moist. Fine but sultry summer weather"],["Yangtze","cT","Spring and autumn","Warm and dry. Travelling highs bring fine weather"],["Equatorial","mE","Summer to autumn (typhoon season)","Very hot and moist. Arrives with typhoons"]]},{"t":"point","x":"In early summer, northeasterly winds from the Okhotsk air mass cross Korea’s Taebaek Mountains and descend as the hot, dry Nopsae wind (a foehn) on the western side. The same air mass can bring very different weather on either side of a mountain range."}]},{"h":"Air masses change as they travel","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Once an air mass leaves its source region, it picks up the character of the surfaces it crosses and gradually changes. This is air-mass modification. It happens thermodynamically (heating or cooling from below, gaining or losing moisture) and dynamically (sinking or rising air)."},{"t":"table","cols":["","k type (cold air over warmer ground)","w type (warm air over colder ground)"],"rows":[["From below","Heated","Cooled"],["Stability","Becomes unstable","Becomes stable"],["Cloud","Cumulus, cumulonimbus","Stratus, stratocumulus, fog"],["Visibility","Good (except in precipitation)","Poor (fog, haze)"],["Ride","Low-level turbulence","Smooth"]]},{"t":"point","x":"This is how heavy winter snow forms. Cold, dry Siberian air crossing the Yellow Sea or the East Sea (Sea of Japan) gains heat and moisture from the warmer water, turns unstable and builds cumulonimbus snow clouds. They dump heavy snow on Korea’s west coast and Ulleungdo, and on the Sea of Japan coast of Japan."}]},{"h":"Fronts: where air masses meet","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The surface where two different air masses meet is the frontal surface, and the line where it touches the ground is the front. Cold air is heavier, so it wedges in underneath the warm air. Fronts lie in troughs of low pressure, where winds from both sides converge and push air upwards, so cloud and rain form easily."},{"t":"fig","id":"met_fronts","cap":"Animated figure: at a cold front (top or left), cold air pushes in underneath and lifts the warm air sharply up a steep slope, building cumulonimbus. At a warm front (bottom or right), warm air slides slowly up over the cold air, and layer cloud spreads from high to low."},{"t":"table","cols":["","Cold front","Warm front"],"rows":[["How it works","Cold air pushes the warm air away","Warm air slides up over the cold air"],["Slope","Steep (about 1/50–1/100)","Gentle (about 1/100–1/300)"],["Cloud","Cumulus, cumulonimbus","Ci → Cs → As → Ns (in order of arrival)"],["Rain","Heavy showers, thunder, hail; short-lived","Steady rain over a wide area for a long time"],["Width of bad weather","Narrow (tens of km near the front)","Wide (hundreds of km ahead of the front)"],["Speed","Fast","Slow"],["After it passes (northern hemisphere)","Temperature and dew point fall, wind veers to the northwest, pressure rises","Temperature rises, wind veers to the south"]]},{"t":"point","warn":true,"x":"Winter rain ahead of a warm front needs care. Rain that forms in the warm layer aloft falls through cold air near the ground and is supercooled below 0 °C; this freezing rain freezes the instant it hits an aircraft or the runway."}]},{"h":"Fronts on the weather chart","blocks":[{"t":"fig","id":"met_front_map","cap":"Animated figure: from the centre of an extratropical low, the warm front runs east and the cold front southwest, with the warm sector between them. Widespread rain lies ahead of the warm front; showers and thunder follow the cold front. Symbols point the way the front is moving."},{"t":"table","cols":["Front","Symbol","Features"],"rows":[["Cold front","Blue triangles","Moves the way the triangles point; weather changes sharply as it passes"],["Warm front","Red semicircles","Moves the way the semicircles point; long spell of bad weather ahead"],["Stationary front","Blue triangles and red semicircles alternating on opposite sides","Hardly moves: the rainy-season fronts (Changma in Korea, Baiu in Japan)"],["Occluded front","Purple triangles and semicircles alternating on the same side","The cold front has caught the warm front; a low at or past its peak"]]},{"t":"check","items":[{"name":"Finding fronts on a chart","x":"A kinked trough in the isobars, a sudden wind shift, a sharp difference in temperature and dew point"},{"name":"On upper-air charts too","x":"Crowded isotherms or equivalent potential temperature lines at 850 hPa mark the frontal zone; an inflow of warm, moist high-θe air warns of heavy rain"}]}]},{"h":"Stationary fronts and the rainy season","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"When warm and cold air are evenly matched, the front barely moves. That is a stationary front, and bad weather can sit over the same place for days. The early-summer rainy-season front (Changma in Korea, Baiu in Japan) and the autumn rain front are the classic examples."},{"t":"rows","items":[{"name":"Features of the rainy-season front","x":"Compared with ordinary fronts, the north–south temperature contrast is small and the moisture contrast large. It is closely linked to the Indian monsoon, the Pacific high and the Okhotsk high"},{"name":"West versus east","x":"Over southern Korea and western Japan, moist air flows in from the south (a moist tongue), cumulonimbus builds and torrential rain is common. Eastern Japan, under the Okhotsk high, tends to get long spells of layer cloud and light rain"},{"name":"Effect on operations","x":"Low cloud, poor visibility and thunderstorms cover a wide area, making alternates hard to choose"}]}]},{"h":"The life of an extratropical cyclone","blocks":[{"t":"fig","id":"met_cyclone_life","cap":"Animated figure: a wave forms on a stationary front (1); the low deepens and the warm sector widens (2); the faster cold front catches the warm front and occlusion begins (3); once occluded, with the warm air lifted aloft, the low weakens (4)."},{"t":"point","x":"An extratropical cyclone draws its energy from the temperature contrast between cold and warm air, deepening as warm air rises and cold air sinks. When a typhoon moves north, meets cold air and begins to develop this way, it has undergone extratropical transition."}]},{"h":"How this connects to dispatch work","blocks":[{"t":"check","items":[{"name":"Timing of frontal passage","x":"Read BECMG and TEMPO in the TAF for when the wind shift or thunderstorms arrive. A wind shift can change the runway in use and delay arrivals"},{"name":"Cold fronts","x":"Lines of cumulonimbus (squall lines), turbulence, hail and low-level wind shear. The bad weather is short-lived, so waiting for it to pass or delaying is a valid option"},{"name":"Warm fronts","x":"Long spells of low cloud and poor visibility, with freezing rain in winter. Choose an alternate outside the same frontal weather"},{"name":"In-flight icing","x":"Frontal cloud between about 0 °C and −20 °C is prone to icing. Check that the anti-icing systems are serviceable"}]},{"t":"link","href":"../22_学科試験過去問/学科試験_過去問.html","x":"Try the Japanese dispatcher exam past papers (aviation weather) on air masses and fronts"}]},{"h":"Where this appears in the exams","blocks":[{"t":"table","cols":["","Korea: flight dispatcher (aviation weather)","Japan: flight dispatcher (aviation weather)"],"rows":[["This lesson","Weather affecting flight; reading weather reports and charts","Air-mass classification and modification, front types and their weather, the rainy-season front, extratropical cyclones"],["Frequent questions","— (the questions are not published)","Air-mass codes and seasons (cPk, mPk, mTw), properties of cold and warm air masses, cold versus warm front weather, frontal slopes, the rainy-season front, what extratropical transition means"]]}]}],"voice":"","terms":[["Air mass","気団","기단"],["Source region","発源地","발원지"],["Air-mass modification","気団の変質","기단의 변질"],["Cold front","寒冷前線","한랭전선"],["Warm front","温暖前線","온난전선"],["Stationary front","停滞前線","정체전선"],["Occluded front","閉塞前線","폐색전선"],["Warm sector","暖域","난역"],["Extratropical cyclone","温帯低気圧","온대저기압"]],"quiz":[{"q":"In the northern hemisphere, what usually happens after a cold front passes?","opts":["Temperature rises, the wind backs southwest and pressure falls","Temperature falls, the wind veers northwest and pressure rises","Temperature and wind stay the same; pressure rises slightly","Pressure keeps falling and stratus and fog spread"],"a":1,"exp":"Behind a cold front lies cold air, so temperature and dew point fall, the wind veers from southerly to westerly or northwesterly, and pressure starts to rise. Warming with a southerly wind is what happens after a warm front passes."},{"q":"As a warm front approaches, in what order do the clouds appear?","opts":["Ns → As → Cs → Ci","Ci → Cs → As → Ns","Cu → Cb → St","St → Cu → Ci"],"a":1,"exp":"A warm front’s surface slopes gently, so far ahead of the front the warm air is only found high up. High cirrus appears first, then cirrostratus and altostratus as it comes closer, and finally rain-bearing nimbostratus near the front."},{"q":"Why does heavy snow fall in winter on Korea’s west coast and the Sea of Japan coast of Japan?","opts":["Warm, moist air cools over a cold sea, becomes stable and turns to snow","Cold Siberian air gains heat and moisture over the warmer sea and becomes unstable","A warm front stalls on the coast and keeps giving light snow","Warm air aloft traps snow clouds under an inversion"],"a":1,"exp":"Cold, dry Siberian air (cPk) crossing the sea is warmed and moistened from below and turns unstable (air-mass modification). The cumulonimbus snow clouds that build then drop heavy snow on the coasts."}],"next":"10-3 Jet Streams, Clear-Air Turbulence and Mountain Waves"});
set("10-3",{"title":"Jet Streams, Clear-Air Turbulence and Mountain Waves: Bumps in a Cloudless Sky","hl":"jet streams and CAT","subtitle":"Where the strong westerlies aloft blow and how they shift with the seasons, what they do to flight times, the rules of thumb for clear-air turbulence (CAT), and the mountain waves and rotors that form downwind of ridges","lead":["At airliner cruising height (about 10 km), there are places where a westerly wind of more than 300 km/h flows like a river. That is the jet stream. Ride it as a tailwind and you arrive early and burn less fuel; fly into it and both time and fuel go up.","Near the jet stream, aircraft can also be shaken suddenly in perfectly clear skies: clear-air turbulence. It is invisible to the eye and to radar, and injures passengers and cabin crew who are not strapped in. This lesson uses figures to look at jet streams, clear-air turbulence and the mountain waves that form downwind of ridges."],"sections":[{"h":"What is a jet stream?","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A jet stream is a long, narrow band of strong westerly wind near the top of the troposphere, close to the tropopause. It is thousands of kilometres long, hundreds wide and a few kilometres deep, and its core often exceeds 300 km/h (about 160 kt). The WMO uses roughly 30 m/s (about 60 kt) as the threshold."},{"t":"fig","id":"met_jet_section","cap":"Animated figure: a cross-section of the northern hemisphere seen from the west (the wind blows into the page). The polar-front jet (about 300 hPa) and the subtropical jet (about 200 hPa) sit near the breaks in the tropopause. The orange patches are where clear-air turbulence is likely, and the aircraft shakes as it passes through them."},{"t":"table","cols":["","Polar-front jet","Subtropical jet"],"rows":[["Height","About 300 hPa (about 9 km)","About 200 hPa (about 12 km)"],["Where it forms","Above the polar front, between cold and warm air","At the edge of the tropics (the poleward end of the Hadley cell)"],["Behaviour","Position and strength change a lot from day to day","Relatively steady"]]},{"t":"point","x":"In winter, the contrast between Siberian cold air and Pacific warm air is large over Japan, and the two jets merge into one of the strongest jet streams in the world, sometimes 150–200 kt or more. In summer it moves north and weakens."}]},{"h":"Jet streams and flight times","blocks":[{"t":"fig","id":"met_jet_map","cap":"Animated figure: in winter (thick band) the jet runs strongly over Japan; in summer (thin band) it shifts north and weakens. Eastbound flights get a tailwind and are faster; westbound flights face a headwind and are slower."},{"t":"check","items":[{"name":"Eastbound versus westbound","x":"Flights from Korea and Japan to North America are shortened by the tailwind; the return flights are lengthened by the headwind. On the same route the difference can reach one to two hours in winter"},{"name":"Choosing route and level","x":"Dispatchers check the upper-wind forecast every day and choose the route and level that make the most of tailwinds and avoid headwinds (over the Pacific, using tracks set daily)"},{"name":"Fuel and weight","x":"On days with strong headwinds more fuel is needed and cargo may have to be cut. Check the wind component in the OFP"}]}]},{"h":"Clear-air turbulence (CAT): bumps without cloud","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Clear-air turbulence occurs away from clouds such as cumulonimbus. Where wind speed or direction changes sharply up and down or side to side (wind shear), waves form at the boundary between layers of air, curl up and break, much like ocean waves breaking on a beach."},{"t":"fig","id":"met_kh","cap":"Animated figure: at the boundary between a fast layer and a slow layer, waves curl up and break (Kelvin–Helmholtz waves). With no cloud, they are invisible to the eye and to radar. The panel on the right (below on phones) gives the rules of thumb."},{"t":"table","cols":["Rule of thumb","Value","Source"],"rows":[["Jet core speed","110 kt or more","FAA AC 00-30C"],["Vertical wind shear","5 kt per 1,000 ft or more","FAA AC 00-30C"],["Horizontal wind shear","40 kt per 150 NM or more","FAA AC 00-30C"],["Isotherm spacing","Closer than 5 °C per 120 NM","Japanese dispatcher exam"]]},{"t":"check","items":[{"name":"Where it is likely","x":"Above the jet core near the sloping tropopause, and below the core on the poleward (cold) side; where the flow curves sharply around troughs and ridges; around cold lows aloft"},{"name":"When it is likely","x":"In winter, when the contrast between cold and warm air is greatest; around Korea and Japan, from autumn to spring"},{"name":"Clues on satellite images","x":"Transverse lines: rows of wavy high cloud at right angles to the jet, usually on its low-latitude side, which mark turbulence"}]},{"t":"point","warn":true,"x":"CAT is hard to forecast and strikes without warning. Dispatchers gather turbulence forecasts and reports from other aircraft (pilot reports) and pass them to the captain and the cabin. For cabin safety, it matters that people keep their seat belts fastened whenever they are seated."}]},{"h":"Mountain waves: waves and rotors downwind of ridges","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"When a strong wind crosses a mountain range at nearly right angles, waves (mountain waves) form in the air downwind. They are most likely with a stable layer above the ridge and wind increasing with height, as when the winter monsoon crosses Korea’s Taebaek Mountains or the mountains of central Japan and Hokkaido."},{"t":"fig","id":"met_mtnwave","cap":"Animated figure: the wind undulates after crossing the ridge, with a cap cloud on the summit and lenticular clouds on the wave crests. Beneath the waves, below ridge height, a spinning rotor (rotor cloud) produces the most severe turbulence. There is a strong downdraft on the lee side."},{"t":"table","cols":["Sign","Where","Caution"],"rows":[["Cap cloud","Sitting on the summit","Strong wind is crossing the ridge"],["Lenticular clouds","On the downwind wave crests","They look still, but the wind inside is strong: up-and-down bumps"],["Rotor cloud","Under the waves, below ridge height","The most severe turbulence; dangerous at low level"],["Strong downdraft","On the lee slope","Can exceed an aircraft’s climb performance"]]},{"t":"point","x":"Mountain-wave turbulence can reach from the ridge tops to above the tropopause and extend 100 miles (about 160 km) or more downwind. Because there may be no cloud, it is also treated as a form of clear-air turbulence."}]},{"h":"How this connects to dispatch work","blocks":[{"t":"check","items":[{"name":"Before departure","x":"Check upper winds (250 and 300 hPa charts, maximum-wind charts) and turbulence forecasts, and share the expected rough segments and levels with the captain and cabin"},{"name":"Route and level","x":"Suggest levels and routes that avoid forecast turbulence; crossing the jet core tends to be bumpy"},{"name":"In flight","x":"Collect turbulence reports from other aircraft, check SIGMETs for severe turbulence, and pass them to following flights"},{"name":"After an encounter","x":"Check for injuries, arrange an aircraft inspection (required after severe turbulence) and prepare reports"}]},{"t":"link","href":"../22_学科試験過去問/学科試験_過去問.html","x":"Try the Japanese dispatcher exam past papers (aviation weather) on jet streams, CAT and mountain waves"}]},{"h":"Where this appears in the exams","blocks":[{"t":"table","cols":["","Korea: flight dispatcher (aviation weather)","Japan: flight dispatcher (aviation weather)"],"rows":[["This lesson","Weather affecting flight (wind systems, turbulence)","Jet streams, clear-air turbulence, mountain waves"],["Frequent questions","— (the questions are not published)","Defining a jet stream (speed, length, width, depth), polar-front versus subtropical jets, CAT threshold values, where mountain-wave turbulence is worst (the rotor)"]]}]}],"voice":"","terms":[["Jet stream","ジェット気流","제트기류"],["Polar-front jet","寒帯前線ジェット","한대전선 제트"],["Subtropical jet","亜熱帯ジェット","아열대 제트"],["Clear-air turbulence (CAT)","晴天乱気流","청천난류"],["Wind shear","ウインドシアー","윈드시어"],["Mountain wave","山岳波","산악파"],["Lenticular cloud","レンズ雲","렌즈구름"],["Rotor","ローター","로터"],["Pilot report (PIREP)","パイロットレポート","조종사 보고"]],"quiz":[{"q":"What vertical wind shear is taken as a rule of thumb for likely CAT?","opts":["1 kt per 1,000 ft or more","5 kt per 1,000 ft or more","50 kt per 1,000 ft or more","Vertical shear does not matter"],"a":1,"exp":"The FAA rule of thumb (AC 00-30C) is vertical shear of 5 kt per 1,000 ft or more. The companion values are horizontal shear of 40 kt per 150 NM or more and a jet core of 110 kt or more."},{"q":"Where is mountain-wave turbulence most severe?","opts":["Inside the cap cloud covering the summit","Just above the smooth top of the high lenticular clouds","Around the rotor (rotor cloud) under the downwind waves","In the updraught on the upwind slope of the ridge"],"a":2,"exp":"It is worst in the rotor, the eddy that forms beneath the downwind waves below ridge height, often marked by a rotor cloud. Because it is at low level, it is especially dangerous for aircraft taking off or landing."},{"q":"In winter, why do flights from Korea and Japan to North America tend to arrive sooner than the return flights?","opts":["The time difference makes them arrive earlier by the clock","Because the jet stream aloft gives them a tailwind","Eastbound flights fly lower on a shorter track","North American airports have more runways and shorter holds"],"a":1,"exp":"The jet stream is a strong westerly, so it is a tailwind for eastbound flights and a headwind for westbound ones. The winter jet near Japan is very strong, which widens the difference between outbound and return flight times."}],"next":"10-4 Thunderstorms, Downbursts and Wind Shear"});
set("10-4",{"title":"Thunderstorms, Downbursts and Wind Shear: The Hazards of Cumulonimbus and How to Avoid Them","hl":"thunderstorms and wind shear","subtitle":"What thunderstorms need and the types, the life cycle (cumulus, mature, dissipating), the hazards of a cumulonimbus, downbursts and microbursts, signs of low-level wind shear, and avoiding storms on radar (the 20-mile rule)","lead":["When cumulonimbus builds near an airport on a summer afternoon, arrivals hold overhead, departures cannot take off, and on the ground lightning stops fuelling and baggage loading. Cumulonimbus is among the most dangerous weather in aviation.","In and around it are severe turbulence, hail, icing, lightning and downbursts that slam into the ground. This lesson uses figures to look at the thunderstorm life cycle, the hazards of a cumulonimbus, the microburst that pushes landing aircraft towards the ground, and how to avoid storms using radar."],"sections":[{"h":"Three ingredients and the types of thunderstorm","blocks":[{"t":"cards","n":3,"items":[{"ic":"💧","name":"Plenty of moisture","x":"The raw material for cloud: summer, or moist air flowing in from a warm sea"},{"ic":"🎈","name":"Unstable air","x":"Lifted air keeps rising (10-1): cold air aloft, heating low down"},{"ic":"⛰","name":"Something to lift it","x":"Sun-heated ground, fronts, mountain slopes, converging winds"}]},{"t":"table","cols":["Type","How it forms","When and where"],"rows":[["Heat (air-mass) thunderstorm","Strong sunshine, or cold winter air moving over a warm sea, heats the lower layer","Summer afternoons inland; winter coasts facing the sea"],["Frontal thunderstorm","Air is lifted at a front","Near cold fronts, at any time of day"],["Cyclonic thunderstorm","Rising air near the centre of a low or typhoon","Around lows and typhoons"],["Heat-frontal thunderstorm","Frontal lifting plus daytime heating","Near fronts in summer"]]}]},{"h":"The life of a thunderstorm","blocks":[{"t":"fig","id":"met_ts_life","cap":"Animated figure: in the cumulus stage there are only updrafts and the cloud grows. In the mature stage, updrafts and downdrafts sit side by side, bringing heavy rain, lightning, hail and gusts (the most dangerous stage). In the dissipating stage only downdrafts remain and the rain eases."},{"t":"point","x":"A single cell lives about an hour, but the downdraft of an old cell can push up a new one, so cells follow one after another (multicell). A very strong, rotating, long-lived updraft is a supercell, which can bring large hail and tornadoes. In the warm sector ahead of a cold front, cumulonimbus can line up as a squall line."}]},{"h":"The hazards of a cumulonimbus","blocks":[{"t":"fig","id":"met_cb_hazards","cap":"Animated figure: hazards around a cumulonimbus. Turbulence extends outside the cloud, and hail can fall outside it, for example under the anvil. At the ground a downburst spreads in all directions, and the gust front (first gust) arrives before the storm."},{"t":"table","cols":["Hazard","Where and what","Effect on aviation"],"rows":[["Turbulence","In and around the cloud; up to 20 miles from a severe storm","Injuries, structural damage"],["Hail","In the cloud, under the anvil, miles from the cloud","Damage to windshields, nose and wings"],["Icing","Supercooled drops between 0 °C and −20 °C","Loss of lift, engine problems"],["Lightning","In cloud and between cloud and ground","Instrument failure; ground work stops"],["Heavy rain","Under the cloud","Poor visibility, standing water on runways (hydroplaning)"],["Downburst","A strong downdraft spreading out at the ground","Pushes aircraft towards the ground during take-off and landing"],["Gust front","Ahead of the storm, at the leading edge of the cold air","Sudden wind shift; runway changes"]]},{"t":"point","x":"The first gust ahead of a storm usually strengthens the wind by about 15 kt and turns it about 40°, but in strong cases the wind can swing 180° and exceed 50–60 kt. Once inside the gust line, temperature falls and pressure rises."}]},{"h":"Downbursts and microbursts","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A downburst is a strong downdraft from a cumulonimbus that hits the ground and spreads out. One less than 4 km across is a microburst; 4 km or more is a macroburst (Dr Tetsuya Fujita’s classification). Wet microbursts come with rain; in dry microbursts the rain evaporates on the way down."},{"t":"fig","id":"met_microburst","cap":"Animated figure: a landing aircraft flies into a microburst. ① The headwind increases, airspeed rises and it floats above the path. ② A strong downdraft pushes it down. ③ The wind turns to a tailwind, airspeed and lift fall, and it sinks below the path. Stage ③ is the most dangerous."},{"t":"check","items":[{"name":"Signs of low-level wind shear","x":"Dust blowing up at the ground (a gust front), wind changing quickly in direction or speed, virga hanging from convective cloud (streaks of rain that do not reach the ground: a clue to a dry microburst)"},{"name":"Not a sign","x":"Ground fog (it forms in calm, stable air)"},{"name":"Airport equipment","x":"Airport weather Doppler radar (best in rain) and Doppler lidar (best at wind changes in clear air) detect microbursts and shear lines. Lidar does not replace radar"},{"name":"Aircraft equipment","x":"Predictive wind-shear alerts from the weather radar, and the wind-shear warning of the ground proximity warning system (GPWS)"}]}]},{"h":"Avoiding thunderstorms on radar","blocks":[{"t":"fig","id":"met_ts_avoid","cap":"Animated figure: a weather radar display. Stay at least 20 miles from strong red echoes and go round on the upwind side; under the anvil downwind there is a risk of hail. Do not fly between two strong echoes unless they are at least 40 miles apart."},{"t":"table","cols":["Rule of thumb (FAA AC 00-24C)","What it says"],"rows":[["Severe storms, intense echoes","Avoid by at least 20 miles"],["Flying between intense echoes","Only if they are at least 40 miles apart"],["Under the anvil","Do not fly there; hail falls away from the cloud"],["A storm approaching the airport","Do not take off or land (sudden wind shifts, low-level turbulence)"],["Tops of 35,000 ft or more, frequent lightning","Treat the storm as severe"]]},{"t":"check","items":[{"name":"If penetration cannot be avoided","x":"Use the weather radar to pick the area of least rain and cross a line of cumulonimbus as close to right angles as possible. Hold attitude on the gyro instruments and, once in, keep going rather than turning back"}]}]},{"h":"How this connects to dispatch work","blocks":[{"t":"check","items":[{"name":"Reading forecasts","x":"TS, CB and TEMPO in the TAF, aerodrome weather information for lightning (about 20 km around the airport), radar and lightning observations. Watch summer afternoons and cold-front timing"},{"name":"Fuel","x":"Add fuel for holding at the destination and for detours around storms"},{"name":"Alternates","x":"Choose alternates that will not be under the same line of storms"},{"name":"Ground operations","x":"When lightning is observed near the airport, ramp fuelling, baggage handling and marshalling stop for safety; share expected delays early"},{"name":"Wind-shear information","x":"When ATC issues a wind-shear or microburst alert, discuss departure and landing timing with the captain"}]},{"t":"link","href":"../22_学科試験過去問/学科試験_過去問.html","x":"Try the Japanese dispatcher exam past papers (aviation weather) on thunderstorms, downbursts and wind shear"}]},{"h":"Where this appears in the exams","blocks":[{"t":"table","cols":["","Korea: flight dispatcher (aviation weather)","Japan: flight dispatcher (aviation weather)"],"rows":[["This lesson","Weather affecting flight (thunderstorms, turbulence)","Thunderstorm life cycle and types, downbursts, low-level wind shear, airport Doppler radar and lidar"],["Frequent questions","— (the questions are not published)","Heat, frontal and cyclonic thunderstorms; features of the first gust; precautions when flying through a storm; signs of low-level wind shear; Doppler lidar; squall lines"]]}]}],"voice":"","terms":[["Cumulonimbus (Cb)","積乱雲","적란운"],["Thunderstorm","雷雨","뇌우"],["Mature stage","成熟期","성숙기"],["Downburst","ダウンバースト","다운버스트"],["Microburst","マイクロバースト","마이크로버스트"],["Gust front","ガストフロント","돌풍전선"],["Low-level wind shear","低層ウインドシア","저층 윈드시어"],["Virga","尾流雲","미류운"],["Squall line","スコールライン","스콜선"]],"quiz":[{"q":"In which stage of a thunderstorm do updrafts and downdrafts sit side by side, with the most heavy rain, lightning and hail?","opts":["Cumulus stage","Mature stage","Dissipating stage","All stages are the same"],"a":1,"exp":"In the mature stage, updrafts sit alongside downdrafts dragged down by the rain, producing heavy rain, lightning, hail and gusts. The cumulus stage has only updrafts; the dissipating stage only downdrafts."},{"q":"When is a microburst most dangerous to a landing aircraft?","opts":["When the headwind increases","When the wind turns to a tailwind","Before entering the area under the cloud","After stopping on the runway"],"a":1,"exp":"First the headwind increases, airspeed rises and the aircraft floats above the path. Then the downdraft pushes it down, and when the wind turns to a tailwind, airspeed and lift drop suddenly and it sinks below the path close to the ground. That is the most dangerous point."},{"q":"Under the FAA rule of thumb (AC 00-24C), how far should you stay from a severe thunderstorm or intense radar echo?","opts":["At least 5 miles","At least 10 miles","At least 20 miles","At least 100 miles"],"a":2,"exp":"Severe storms and intense echoes should be avoided by at least 20 miles, because turbulence and hail extend beyond the cloud. Fly between two intense echoes only if they are at least 40 miles apart."}],"next":"10-5 Typhoons"});
set("10-5",{"title":"Typhoons: How They Work, How They Move, Reading the Forecast and Preparing Operations","hl":"typhoons","subtitle":"What counts as a typhoon (maximum wind of about 17 m/s or more), how it forms and its structure (eye, eyewall, spiral rain bands), why the right side is dangerous, tracks and forecast circles, intensity and size, and the effect on airports and operations","lead":["From summer into autumn, the biggest weather event for Korean and Japanese airlines is the typhoon. Airports close, hundreds of cancellations are decided the day before, and aircraft may be flown out to safe airports.","Where are typhoons born, how do they move, and why is the right-hand side dangerous? What does the forecast circle mean? This lesson uses figures to explain how typhoons work and how to read the forecast, and looks at how dispatchers and airport stations prepare."],"sections":[{"h":"What is a typhoon?","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A typhoon is a tropical cyclone in the northwest Pacific or the South China Sea whose maximum wind near the centre (10-minute mean) has reached about 17 m/s (34 kt) or more. The Japan and Korea meteorological agencies use the same threshold. The same kind of storm is called a hurricane in the Atlantic and northeast Pacific, and a cyclone in the Indian Ocean and South Pacific."},{"t":"fig","id":"met_ty_scale","cap":"Animated figure: the Japan Meteorological Agency grades typhoon intensity in three steps by maximum wind, and size in two steps by the radius of winds of 15 m/s or more. The line at the bottom is the length of Japan (about 2,000 km), for comparison with a very large typhoon."},{"t":"table","cols":["","Japan (JMA)","International (WMO categories)"],"rows":[["Becomes a typhoon","About 17 m/s (34 kt) or more","Tropical storm (TS): 34 kt or more"],["Strong","33 m/s (64 kt) or more","Typhoon (TY): 64 kt or more"],["Very strong","44 m/s (85 kt) or more","—"],["Violent","54 m/s (105 kt) or more","—"],["Strong-wind / storm area","Winds of 15 m/s or more / 25 m/s or more","—"]]},{"t":"note","x":"* 34–47 kt is a tropical storm (TS) and 48–63 kt a severe tropical storm (STS); these English categories also appear in aviation weather products such as SIGMETs. The Korea Meteorological Administration uses the same thresholds with different names: 25–33 m/s is “moderate”, 33–44 m/s “strong”, 44–54 m/s “very strong” and 54 m/s or more “super strong”."}]},{"h":"How typhoons form and their structure","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Typhoons form over tropical seas with a surface temperature of 26–27 °C or more. The heat released when huge amounts of evaporated water vapour rise and condense into cloud (latent heat) is what powers them. They hardly ever form right next to the equator (within about 5°), where the effect of the Earth’s rotation is too weak."},{"t":"fig","id":"met_ty_section","cap":"Animated figure: a cross-section of a typhoon. Moist air is drawn in towards the centre at low level, shoots up in the eyewall and flows out at the top. Inside the eye the air sinks, so the wind is light and the sky clear. The centre is warmer than its surroundings."},{"t":"table","cols":["Part","What it is like"],"rows":[["Eye","A few tens of kilometres across; sinking air, light wind, little cloud"],["Eyewall","A wall of towering cumulonimbus around the eye; the strongest wind and rain"],["Spiral rain bands","Bands of rain cloud wrapped around the outside; heavy rain and gusts as each one passes"],["Upper cirrus","Outflowing air spreads out and looks like a white disc on satellite images"]]},{"t":"point","warn":true,"x":"When the eye passes overhead, the wind can drop and the sky clear. The typhoon has not gone: once the eye passes, violent winds return from the opposite direction."}]},{"h":"The dangerous right-hand side","blocks":[{"t":"fig","id":"met_ty_plan","cap":"Animated figure: seen from above, a northern-hemisphere typhoon spins anticlockwise. On the right of its track, the storm’s forward speed adds to the circulating wind (the dangerous semicircle); on the left it subtracts (the navigable semicircle)."},{"t":"p","x":"If the circulating wind is 40 m/s and the typhoon moves north at 10 m/s, the wind on the right (east) side is about 50 m/s and on the left (west) side about 30 m/s. The faster a typhoon races along on the westerlies, the stronger its right side becomes."}]},{"h":"Tracks and forecast circles","blocks":[{"t":"fig","id":"met_ty_track","cap":"Animated figure: typhoons form at low latitudes, move northwest around the Pacific high, turn northeast at its western edge (recurvature) and then speed up on the westerlies. The white dotted circles are forecast circles, growing larger further ahead."},{"t":"check","items":[{"name":"The wind that steers it","x":"The steering flow is often close to the wind near 700 hPa before recurvature and near 500 hPa afterwards"},{"name":"Forecast circle","x":"The area with a 70% chance of containing the centre at that time. A bigger circle means more uncertainty, not a bigger typhoon"},{"name":"Storm warning area","x":"The area that may come within the storm area (25 m/s or more) if the centre moves anywhere inside the forecast circle"},{"name":"Extratropical transition","x":"Moving north into cold air, it becomes a frontal extratropical low. The wind does not necessarily ease, and the area of strong wind may grow"}]},{"t":"note","x":"* On JMA normals (1991–2020), about 25 typhoons form each year, about 12 approach Japan and about 3 make landfall. Around three a year affect Korea."}]},{"h":"Effects on airports and operations","blocks":[{"t":"table","cols":["Effect","Details"],"rows":[["Crosswinds and gusts","Take-off and landing are impossible beyond each type’s crosswind limit"],["Closures and cancellations","Most flights are cancelled while the storm area covers the airport, often decided the day before"],["Storm surge and flooding","Coastal airports can flood (Kansai International Airport flooded in Typhoon Jebi in 2018)"],["Evacuating aircraft","Parked aircraft are flown to airports out of the typhoon’s path so they are not blown about or damaged"],["Ground preparations","Tie down ground equipment and vehicles, stow boarding bridges, stop ramp work"],["Positioning crews and aircraft","Plan where crews and aircraft will be so operations can restart after the storm"]]},{"t":"check","items":[{"name":"Dispatchers","x":"Use the forecast circles and the timing of the storm area to decide which flights to cancel, delay or reroute; choose alternates away from the track and add holding fuel"},{"name":"Airport stations","x":"Set up a typhoon task force, handle cancellation notices and rebooking, arrange hotels and transport, and keep the ramp safe"}]}]},{"h":"Where this appears in the exams","blocks":[{"t":"table","cols":["","Korea: flight dispatcher (aviation weather)","Japan: flight dispatcher (aviation weather)"],"rows":[["This lesson","Weather affecting flight (tropical weather, strong wind)","Typhoon definition, structure and tracks; forecast circles and storm warning areas; the dangerous semicircle"],["Frequent questions","— (the questions are not published)","Conditions for formation (sea temperature), eye and eyewall, warm core, winds on the right side, steering flow (700 and 500 hPa), what the forecast circle means (70%), sea temperature after a typhoon passes"]]}]}],"voice":"","terms":[["Typhoon","台風","태풍"],["Tropical cyclone","熱帯低気圧","열대저기압"],["Eyewall","眼の壁","눈벽"],["Warm core","暖気核","온난핵"],["Dangerous semicircle","危険半円","위험반원"],["Recurvature","転向","전향"],["Forecast circle","予報円","예보원"],["Storm area (25 m/s or more)","暴風域","폭풍역"],["Extratropical transition","温帯低気圧化","온대저기압화"]],"quiz":[{"q":"What maximum wind makes a tropical cyclone a “typhoon” for the Japan Meteorological Agency?","opts":["About 10 m/s (20 kt) or more","About 17 m/s (34 kt) or more","About 33 m/s (64 kt) or more","About 54 m/s (105 kt) or more"],"a":1,"exp":"A tropical cyclone in the northwest Pacific or South China Sea becomes a typhoon when its maximum wind reaches about 17 m/s (34 kt). 33 m/s or more is described as “strong” and 54 m/s or more as “violent”."},{"q":"What does a forecast circle in a typhoon forecast mean?","opts":["Where storm-force winds (25 m/s+) blow at that time","The area with a 70% chance of containing the typhoon’s centre at that time","Where strong winds (15 m/s+) blow at that time","A larger circle means a bigger typhoon"],"a":1,"exp":"The forecast circle is where the centre is expected to be at that time, with a 70% probability. Circles grow larger further ahead because the forecast becomes less certain, not because the typhoon grows."},{"q":"On which side of a northern-hemisphere typhoon is the wind usually stronger?","opts":["Left of the track","Right of the track","Directly over the centre (the eye)","Both sides are the same"],"a":1,"exp":"Winds circulate anticlockwise in the northern hemisphere, so on the right of the track the typhoon’s forward speed adds to the circulating wind. This is the dangerous semicircle; on the left the speeds subtract, giving the navigable semicircle."}],"next":"10-6 Reading Upper-Air Charts"});
set("10-6",{"title":"Reading Upper-Air Charts: Winds, Temperatures, Troughs and Ridges Aloft","hl":"upper-air charts","subtitle":"Pressure-level heights (850–200 hPa) and what each is used for, how height contours relate to wind, troughs and ridges, reading wind barbs and dew-point depression, vorticity, and cold-air thresholds: directly tied to cruising winds and forecasting bad weather","lead":["Where a surface chart shows the weather near the ground, upper-air charts show the air where airliners fly. Dispatchers read cruising-level winds and temperatures from them and work out where turbulence and thunderstorms are likely.","Until you know them, upper-air charts look like nothing but lines and symbols, yet the rules for reading them are few. This lesson goes through pressure-level heights, height contours and wind, wind barbs and vorticity one at a time with animated figures, and rounds off Part 10, Aviation Weather Basics."],"sections":[{"h":"What is an upper-air chart?","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"An upper-air chart shows conditions on a surface of equal pressure, such as 850 hPa or 500 hPa. Instead of pressure, it joins points at the same height of that pressure surface (height contours, in metres). The data come from radiosondes carried aloft by balloons, launched worldwide twice a day at the same time (09:00 and 21:00 Korean and Japanese time)."},{"t":"fig","id":"met_levels","cap":"Animated figure: as the radiosonde rises, each pressure level lights up in turn. Airliners cruise between roughly 300 hPa and 200 hPa."},{"t":"table","cols":["Level","Height (standard atmosphere)","Main use"],"rows":[["850 hPa","About 1,500 m (5,000 ft)","Low-level temperature and moisture; rain or snow; inflow of warm, moist air"],["700 hPa","About 3,000 m (10,000 ft)","Cloud cover from dew-point depression; rising air; a common icing level"],["500 hPa","About 5,500 m (18,000 ft)","Movement of troughs and ridges, cold air aloft: the ‘steering wheel’ of the weather"],["300 hPa","About 9,000 m (FL300)","Jet stream; winds and temperatures at cruising level"],["250 and 200 hPa","About 10,400 m and 11,800 m (FL340, FL390)","Jet core; near the tropopause"]]}]},{"h":"Height contours and wind","blocks":[{"t":"fig","id":"met_upper_chart","cap":"Animated figure: a 500 hPa chart. Heights are lower (colder) to the north and higher (warmer) to the south. The wind blows along the contours with the low heights on its left, and it is strongest where the lines are closest together. Weather is poor ahead of (east of) the trough and fine under the ridge."},{"t":"check","items":[{"name":"Wind direction","x":"Aloft, the wind blows almost parallel to the contours (geostrophic wind), with the low heights on the left in the northern hemisphere, so mid-latitude winds aloft are usually westerly"},{"name":"Wind speed","x":"The closer the contours, the stronger the wind; the jet stream is where they are most crowded"},{"name":"Height and temperature","x":"A column of warm air expands, so contour values are high; over cold air they are low"},{"name":"Trough","x":"Where the lines dip south. Ahead of it (east) rising air brings cloud and rain; behind it (west) sinking air brings clearer skies. Troughs normally move from west to east"},{"name":"Ridge","x":"Where the lines bulge north; sinking air favours fine weather"},{"name":"Cold low (cut-off low)","x":"A trough pinched off into a closed low. Strong cold air aloft keeps thunderstorms, hail and turbulence going for a long time"}]}]},{"h":"Reading a station plot: wind barbs and dew-point depression","blocks":[{"t":"fig","id":"met_barbs","cap":"Animated figure: add up the pennant (50 kt), long barbs (10 kt) and short barbs (5 kt). The wind blows from the direction the shaft points. Beside the station, the red figure is the temperature and the blue figure the dew-point depression (temperature − dew point)."},{"t":"table","cols":["Symbol","Meaning"],"rows":[["Short barb","5 kt"],["Long barb","10 kt"],["Pennant (triangle)","50 kt"],["Temperature","Plotted beside the station (°C)"],["Dew-point depression (T − Td)","The difference between temperature and dew point; under 3 °C marks moist air where cloud is likely, shaded on the chart"]]},{"t":"note","x":"* Where temperature and depression are plotted can vary between chart types; check the chart’s legend."}]},{"h":"Vorticity: how strongly the air spins","blocks":[{"t":"fig","id":"met_vort","cap":"Animated figure: in a trough the air turns anticlockwise (positive vorticity); in a ridge it turns clockwise (negative vorticity). Ahead of the trough, where the wind carries positive vorticity, the air rises and cloud forms."},{"t":"p","x":"Vorticity measures how strongly the air is rotating; in the northern hemisphere anticlockwise is positive. On 500 hPa vorticity charts, large positive values mark troughs and lows, and to their east (ahead) rising motion strengthens and lows tend to deepen. Vorticity is positive on the north side of the jet stream and negative on the south side, with a zero line along the core of strongest wind."}]},{"h":"Useful threshold values","blocks":[{"t":"table","cols":["Level and element","Threshold (values often used in Japanese forecasting)","Meaning"],"rows":[["850 hPa temperature","−6 °C or below","Snow likely even on low ground"],["500 hPa temperature","−30 °C or below (−36 °C or below in winter)","Strong cold air aloft: unstable air, thunderstorms or heavy snow"],["700 hPa dew-point depression","Under 3 °C","Cloud widespread or forming"],["850 hPa equivalent potential temperature","High values flowing in, with crowded lines","Risk of heavy rain (frontal zone)"]]},{"t":"note","x":"* Thresholds vary with season and region. Korean forecasting uses the same ideas, for example judging rain or snow from the 850 hPa temperature."}]},{"h":"How this connects to dispatch work","blocks":[{"t":"check","items":[{"name":"Cruising-level winds and temperatures","x":"Use the 300–200 hPa winds to pick routes and levels that exploit tailwinds and avoid headwinds; temperatures above standard affect engine thrust and maximum altitude"},{"name":"Forecasting bad weather","x":"From the position and movement of 500 hPa troughs and cold lows, work out where and when thunderstorms and turbulence are likely"},{"name":"Icing","x":"Watch for icing near 700 hPa where the dew-point depression is small and the temperature is between 0 °C and −20 °C"},{"name":"Together with other charts","x":"Read cross-sections (10-1), jet stream and maximum-wind charts (10-3), and emagrams and stability indices (10-1) alongside them"}]},{"t":"link","href":"../22_学科試験過去問/学科試験_過去問.html","x":"Try the Japanese dispatcher exam past papers (aviation weather) on upper-air charts, vorticity and cross-sections"}]},{"h":"Where this appears in the exams","blocks":[{"t":"table","cols":["","Korea: flight dispatcher (aviation weather)","Japan: flight dispatcher (aviation weather)"],"rows":[["This lesson","Weather reports and chart interpretation","Upper-air charts, height contours and wind, vorticity, cross-sections, equivalent potential temperature"],["Frequent questions","— (the questions are not published)","Pressure-level heights, contours and wind, trough movement and weather, the sign of vorticity and its link to the jet, reading 850 hPa equivalent potential temperature charts, reading cross-sections"]]}]}],"voice":"","terms":[["Upper-air chart","高層天気図","고층 일기도"],["Constant-pressure surface","等圧面","등압면"],["Height contour","等高度線","등고도선"],["Trough","気圧の谷（トラフ）","기압골(트로프)"],["Ridge","気圧の尾根（リッジ）","기압마루(리지)"],["Dew-point depression","湿数","습수"],["Wind barb","矢羽根","바람 깃"],["Vorticity","渦度","와도"],["Cold low (cut-off low)","寒冷渦","한랭와"]],"quiz":[{"q":"Roughly how high is the 500 hPa surface in the standard atmosphere?","opts":["About 1,500 m","About 3,000 m","About 5,500 m","About 9,000 m"],"a":2,"exp":"500 hPa is about 5,500 m (18,000 ft), around the middle of the atmosphere. 850 hPa is about 1,500 m, 700 hPa about 3,000 m and 300 hPa about 9,000 m."},{"q":"A wind barb has one pennant, one long barb and one short barb. What is the wind speed?","opts":["16 kt","35 kt","65 kt","115 kt"],"a":2,"exp":"A pennant is 50 kt, a long barb 10 kt and a short barb 5 kt, so 50 + 10 + 5 = 65 kt."},{"q":"On a 500 hPa chart, where is the weather most likely to be poor?","opts":["Directly under a ridge","Ahead of (east of) a trough","Behind (west of) a trough","Where there are no contours"],"a":1,"exp":"Ahead of a trough, positive vorticity is carried in and air rises, so cloud and rain form easily. Behind a trough and under a ridge, sinking air favours fine weather."}],"next":"Part 11 Air Navigation and Calculations: 11-1 Types of Speed"});
})(window.ARTS);
